messages:
anna-cs: FYI customer hitting rerank endpoint; they get a 422 and when it accepts the payload all returned scores are 0. payload attached from their SDK call.

anna-cs: payload (redacted):
```json
{
  "user_id": "acct-123",
  "docs": [
    {
      "id": "d1",
      "text": "First doc text",
      "query": "how to bake"  // customer accidentally put query inside each doc
    },
    {
      "id": "d2",
      "text": "Second doc"
    }
  ],
  "options": {
    "top_k": 5
  }
}
```

anna-cs: Service returned 422 on first attempt: "Unprocessable entity: expected top-level \"query\" or \"candidates\" array." then on a retry (server accepted) we got back scores all 0. Customer says this worked previously.

devon-support: Can you paste the exact curl you used that produced the 422 and the subsequent accepted response? Also which SDK version?

anna-cs: curl they used (from their support log):
```bash
curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/rerank \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-xxx' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"user_id":"acct-123","docs":[{"id":"d1","text":"First doc","query":"how to bake"}],"options":{"top_k":5}}'
```

anna-cs: 422 payload response body: `{"error":"Field \"docs\" not expected in this mode; top-level \"query\" missing"}`. When they removed the per-doc \"query\" and instead supplied `\"query\": \"how to bake\"` at the root it returned meaningful non-zero scores.

mike-sdk: This is a classic case of the server inferring mode from fields. Current behavior: if you send `candidates` at top level we treat input as candidate-list mode; if you send `docs` we treat it as doc-collection mode which expects a separate query placement. The server *does not* hunt for `query` deeply inside docs to avoid ambiguous shadowing; only top-level `query` is used for rerank scoring. If customers accidentally put `query` inside each doc, it'll be ignored and some legacy paths produce zero scores.

mike-sdk: Quick repro I ran locally:
```json
// request A -> returns scores
{
  "query": "how to bake",
  "candidates": [ {"id":"c1","text":"Bake bread"} ]
}
// request B -> same text but query nested in docs -> accepted but returns zeros
{
  "docs": [ {"id":"d1","text":"Bake bread","query":"how to bake"} ]
}
```

mike-sdk: SDK v0.9.2 used to flatten/nest payloads differently in a refactor; older versions sometimes moved the query to root. Which SDK version is the customer on?

anna-cs: Customer on node-sdk 0.9.2. They said it worked last month and they didn't change code; looks like server-side change to stricter validation rolled out.

eva-ml: From the model/serving side, if query is not provided at top-level we treat it as an implicit noop (no query => no signal) and fallback scoring can produce near-zero or tie scores. This explains the zeroes. The 422 occurs because our schema validation now rejects mixed shapes where docs contain a top-level-like field (we added stricter checks to avoid ambiguous semantics).

eva-ml: Two paths: 1) recommended — ask them to move the query to top-level `query` and supply `candidates` or `docs` appropriately; 2) short-term — server can accept nested `docs[i].query` by promoting to top-level for backward compatibility, but that needs an ENG change and a spec note.

anna-cs: Customer can do the top-level change; they asked for a minimal patch to their node code. I drafted this snippet to rewrite their outgoing payload: 
```js
// before sending
if (payload.docs && payload.docs[0] && payload.docs[0].query && !payload.query) {
  payload.query = payload.docs[0].query;
  payload.docs.forEach(d => delete d.query);
}
```

sre-oncall: Note: be careful with auto-promoting in server because some users legitimately have a `query` field in doc metadata. We should only promote if top-level `query` missing and all docs with `query` share the identical string. Otherwise it's ambiguous.

mike-sdk: I'll open a quick PR to the node-sdk that warns when nesting `query` is detected and optionally auto-promotes (opt-in). I'll also add an inline warning in the client-side schema validation.

anna-cs: For the customer response I'll include the exact repro steps: 1) confirm SDK version; 2) either move `query` to root or apply the rewrite above; 3) retry rerank and paste response body if still zeros.

devon-support: If they can't change client, escalate to eng with their sample payload + logs. Tagging the infra change as a low-risk compatibility fix (promote identical nested queries).

anna-cs: I'll reply to the customer now with the workaround and ask if they can upgrade SDK. I'll attach the sample curl and code snippet. Thanks all :thumbsup:
